WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.960
Marvel's Iron Fist, the final season of super-powered
Netflix action until the Defenders crossover

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.070
miniseries later this year, was highly anticipated
by fans of Marvel's "street level" heroes.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.840
And then it actually came out and fans discovered
that Danny Rand's superpower isn't his Iron

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:15.940
Fist at all.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:19.470
Rather, it's the ability to cause intense
disappointment and boredom.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.259
So just what really went wrong with Iron Fist?

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:24.989
Who is Danny Rand?

00:00:24.989 --> 00:00:29.089
Each of the other Defenders have well-defined
character traits: Matt Murdock struggles with

00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:32.050
his Catholic guilt while dealing harshly with
the scum of the Earth.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:36.210
Jessica Jones is an alcoholic survivor of
abuse who tries and often fails to make good

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.210
choices.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.520
And Luke Cage carries the injustice and conflict
of his past into every situation.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.290
And then there's Danny, whose only defined
traits seem to be confusion and stupidity.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.010
Worse, his motivations are a complete mystery
even to himself.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.440
Several times during the course of the show,
characters ask Danny point blank why he abandoned

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.140
his role as the protector of the mystical
city of K'un-Lun in order to return to New

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:58.640
York.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.320
And… he never has an answer, because he
doesn't seem to know.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:07.720
When even the characters don't know why they're
doing stuff, it becomes pretty hard for fans

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.469
to get invested in the outcome.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:11.060
"What happened?"

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.820
"I'm not sure I can explain."

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.289
Poor fight sequences

00:01:17.289 --> 00:01:21.439
The total lack of character development could
be forgiven to an extent if the fight scenes

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:22.549
were awesome.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.359
After all, this is Iron Fist!

00:01:24.359 --> 00:01:26.600
Fight scenes are what Iron Fist does!

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.179
But instead, despite the fact that the entire
show is supposed to be about super kung fu

00:01:30.179 --> 00:01:34.960
killers with magic powers, the fight sequences
are amazing...ly boring.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.009
No it only does Danny almost never actually
use the Iron Fist, his fight scenes are consistently

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:42.009
subpar.

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:45.759
That's not only compared to other Marvel Netflix
shows like Daredevil, but even when compared

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:48.780
to fight scenes in Iron Fist featuring Colleen
Wing.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.979
That suggests the problem may lie with star
Finn Jones, who doesn't quite have the physical

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.069
presence to pull off the whole "Immortal Weapon"
thing.

00:01:56.069 --> 00:02:00.450
Co-star Jessica Henwick, who plays Colleen
Wing, seems much better suited to the show's

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:05.340
brief outbursts of action, making her cage
fight sequences a rare highlight.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.889
Poor plotting and pacing

00:02:06.889 --> 00:02:10.410
As underwhelming as the action can be, it
would help if there were more of it — or,

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.530
at the very least, if the plot didn't take
so long to develop.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:16.470
The show takes absolutely forever to get anywhere.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.810
Several episodes are devoted to boardroom
strategy sessions at Rand Enterprises, Danny's

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.860
family's company, instead of the stuff that
everyone came to see: mystical kung fu action.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.220
Danny's fumbling attempts to prove his identity
drag on and on, boring viewers and making

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.430
Danny look like kind of an idiot.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.670
Meanwhile, most of the screen time isn't devoted
to Iron Fist at all, but rather the scheming

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.910
in-fighting in the family who took over the
business in his absence.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.930
The show doesn't seem to know what it is,
or who it's about.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:47.960
While the show's pace picks up a little after
the first several episodes, it never overcomes

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.100
its plotting problems, leaving questions big
and small unanswered and indulging in unearned

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.880
and often random plot twists.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:59.060
A multi-episode arc that involves Danny and
the gang chasing an enemy to China, for example,

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.310
requires numerous logic leaps and a severe
suspension of disbelief to make any sense

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.310
at all.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.090
It's not just a poor story.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.640
It's poorly told.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.030
Crossover characters

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:13.010
The characters of Jeri Hogarth and Claire
Temple from previous Netflix series both have

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.620
supporting roles, and they immediately class
up any scene they're in.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.330
But this actually does the show no favors,
because Hogarth's steely charisma and Temple's

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.230
reluctant heroism nicely illustrate the lack
of defined character traits for the main cast.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:29.080
They serve as unfortunate reminders of better-written
shows.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:33.560
"I really didn't realize there was going to
be so much emotional honesty."

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:34.820
"Right."

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.910
And while Claire's tendency to point out the
story's plot holes as they crop up injects

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:43.450
a dose of humor into a series that takes itself
way too seriously, it also highlights the

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.320
show's shortcomings while making Claire seem
like a visitor from a totally different, much

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.320
better series.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.820
Which… she totally is.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.210
"Have you stopped to consider that this idea
totally sucks?"

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:56.750
"It doesn't suck, okay?"

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.090
"Kinda harsh, Claire."

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.320
"Not really!"

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.900
Moral confusion

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.820
As we're constantly reminded throughout the
series, Danny was raised to be the Iron Fist,

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.320
the Immortal Weapon.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.150
His job is to defend K'un Lun from the Hand,
the villainous group of ninja assassins who

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.770
plague Daredevil on his show as well.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:20.060
Despite being trained from childhood to destroy
the Hand wherever he finds them, Danny finds

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.290
himself morally conflicted several times over
whether to kill his enemies.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.760
That's nice, but it doesn't feel organic to
the character at all.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.190
It feels like a moral conundrum forced onto
Danny by writers confused over how to inject

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:37.070
internal drama into a character they don't
have a real handle on.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:38.780
The diversity problem

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.260
Somewhere in Iron Fist is the potential for
an interesting discourse about privilege.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.530
That conversation never actually happens on
the show, though.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.060
And it's too bad because it desperately needs
it.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:54.030
Danny comes off as beyond entitled, using
his wealth, status, and family name as a bigger

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.150
weapon than his magic fist, and with even
less thought.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.940
That in and of itself might not have been
an insurmountable problem given that the most

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:05.370
popular character in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, Tony Stark, is also a rich, white

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.370
jerk.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.040
"What did I tell you would happen if you ate
the last donut with red, white, and blue sprinkles?"

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.820
"I'm a little foggy on it, but I think it
was something like 'raining down hellfire.'"

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.720
But Stark is self aware.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.180
He knows he's a jerk!

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.180
That's why we love him.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.820
"Can you feel that?

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.820
We're done here.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.870
Move out of the way or I'm gonna run you over.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:24.870
Bye kid"

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.820
Danny Rand, on the other hand, is not self
aware at all.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:31.400
And let's also consider the fact that much
of the show's pre-release buzz centered on

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.120
questions about cultural appropriation and
whitewashing, since Marvel decided to cast

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.330
a white actor in the role of an Asian savior
figure.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:42.840
The series needed to address those issues
— instead of making them worse.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.760
The first big casting misfire

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.340
Most fans would agree that Marvel Studios
has an unbelievable track record with its

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.230
casting of major characters.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:56.040
From Robert Downey, Jr.'s complete embodiment
of Tony Stark to Tom Holland's pitch-perfect

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.150
Peter Parker, they've been remarkably sure-handed.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.440
And the Netflix series have been no exception
— until now.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:07.130
Unfortunately, the early skepticism of Jones'
casting turned out to be well-warranted.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.070
He may be a fine actor, but he was woefully
miscast in this role.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:15.670
There's never an onscreen moment that truly
feels like the Iron Fist from the comics,

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:20.010
except for one very brief scene in which Danny
is shown old film footage of a previous — and

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.610
much cooler — Iron Fist in action.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:27.540
Whoever that dude was, we'd love to have seen
more of him, and more of that kind of frenetic

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.830
butt kicking action instead of the soggy mess
we got.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.800
Still, there's reason to hope that The Defenders
can right the ship.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.890
Showrunners Marco Ramirez and Douglas Petrie
called the shots on Daredevil's second season,

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:41.900
and there's plenty of opportunity for Danny's
character to grow within the dynamic of his

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.169
team of super-buddies.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:47.450
Perhaps we'll see a more disciplined take
on the Immortal Iron Fist when the eight-episode

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:49.949
Defenders series makes its way to Netflix...

00:06:49.949 --> 00:06:51.210
Thanks for watching!

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.770
